//!
//! # 8254 Programmable Interval Timer
//!
//! A reference clock with a known frequency, used once to measure the local
//! APIC timer.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! The legacy 8254 programmable interval timer is used only as a reference
//! clock. Its input runs at 1193182 Hz on every PC, which makes it the
//! simplest way to measure the local APIC timer, whose frequency is not
//! written down anywhere.
//!
//! Channel 2 is used because its gate and output are visible through port
//! 0x61 and it has no interrupt line: the kernel loads a count, opens the
//! gate and polls the output. In mode 0 (interrupt on terminal count) the
//! output goes low when the count is loaded and high when it reaches zero.
//!
//! ```text
//!   OUT2 (port 0x61 bit 5)
//!
//!   high ┐                                            ┌──────────
//!        │ count loaded, gate closed                  │
//!   low  └──────────┬─────────────────────────────────┘
//!                   │ gate opened: counts down from `count` at 1193182 Hz
//!                   ◄────── count / 1193182 seconds ──►  output goes high
//! ```
//!
//! ## Ports
//!
//! | Port   | Name        | Use                                              |
//! |--------|-------------|--------------------------------------------------|
//! | `0x42` | channel 2 data | write the 16-bit count, low byte first, then high byte |
//! | `0x43` | mode/command | select channel, access mode and counting mode  |
//! | `0x61` | system control B | bit 0 gate 2, bit 1 speaker data, bit 5 OUT2 status |
//!
//! Command byte `0xB0`: bits 7-6 = `10` channel 2, bits 5-4 = `11` low byte
//! then high byte, bits 3-1 = `000` mode 0, bit 0 = `0` binary counting.
//!
//! See concept doc 12 and the OSDev wiki page "Programmable Interval Timer".

import _ from "./io";

/// The PIT API: a countdown on channel 2 that can be polled.
namespace Arch::Pit {
  /// The frequency of the PIT input clock in Hz.
  const FREQUENCY: u64 = 1193182;

  /// Data port of channel 2.
  const CHANNEL_2_DATA: u16 = 0x42;

  /// Mode/command register.
  const COMMAND: u16 = 0x43;

  /// System control port B: the channel 2 gate and the speaker control live
  /// here, and OUT2 can be read.
  const CONTROL: u16 = 0x61;

  /// Channel 2, low byte then high byte, mode 0, binary counting.
  const CHANNEL_2_MODE_0: u8 = 0xB0;

  /// Port `0x61` bit 0: the channel 2 gate. The counter runs while it is set.
  const GATE_ENABLE: u8 = 0x01;

  /// Port `0x61` bit 1: connects channel 2 to the PC speaker. Kept clear so the
  /// measurement is silent.
  const SPEAKER_ENABLE: u8 = 0x02;

  /// Port `0x61` bit 5: the channel 2 output pin.
  const OUTPUT_HIGH: u8 = 0x20;

  /// Loads `count` into channel 2 and starts it counting down. The speaker
  /// stays off. The countdown takes `count / FREQUENCY` seconds.
  function startCountdown(count: u16): void {
    let control: u8 = Arch::Port::readByte(CONTROL) & ~(GATE_ENABLE | SPEAKER_ENABLE);

    Arch::Port::writeByte(CONTROL, control);
    Arch::Port::writeByte(COMMAND, CHANNEL_2_MODE_0);
    Arch::Port::writeByte(CHANNEL_2_DATA, (count & 0xFF) as u8);
    Arch::Port::writeByte(CHANNEL_2_DATA, (count >> 8) as u8);
    Arch::Port::writeByte(CONTROL, control | GATE_ENABLE);
  }

  /// True once the countdown started by `startCountdown` reached zero.
  function hasExpired(): boolean {
    return (Arch::Port::readByte(CONTROL) & OUTPUT_HIGH) != 0;
  }

  /// Closes the channel 2 gate again.
  function stop(): void {
    Arch::Port::writeByte(CONTROL, Arch::Port::readByte(CONTROL) & ~GATE_ENABLE);
  }
}
